# ATI PH environment — pick a profile, do not assemble one.
#
# This file used to hold every variable with alternatives commented out beside them,
# which meant a developer had to work out which lines applied to their situation before
# they could trust any of them. Reading configuration should not require judgement.
#
# There are three profiles. Each is complete: every variable ATI PH reads is written
# out, including the ones whose value equals the code default. Copy one to `.env` and
# the file tells you what the process will do.
#
#   .env.local.example        local development
#                             email:  manual connectivity test only
#                             worker: DISABLED
#
#   .env.test.example         shared test environment
#                             email:  controlled pilot, one internal mailbox
#                             worker: enabled, delivers nothing
#
#   .env.production.example   production
#                             email:  AUTOMATIC — sends to real client recipients
#                             worker: enabled
#
# On Windows:
#
#   copy .env.local.example .env
#
# `src/config/server-env.ts` validates the result and refuses combinations that cannot
# mean what they say — automatic delivery armed against a non-SMTP transport, the test
# and pilot commands both enabled, delivery armed while the worker is disabled. A file
# edited into one of those states fails at boot with the contradiction named, rather
# than starting and quietly doing something else.
#
# `docs/ENVIRONMENT-PROFILES.md` is the reference: what each profile may do, which
# variable decides it, and what has to be true before the next profile is used.
